Based on my review of the refactoring diff, here are the altitude findings:

```json
[
  {
    "file": "src/lib/services/PDFExportService.ts",
    "line": 507,
    "summary": "drawImageUnavailable takes a `wrap: boolean` flag that switches between drawWrappedText and drawText. The three call sites pass true (no image path, line 482), false (fetch returned null, line 489), true (catch, line 499). This is a shared helper with a behavior-switching flag that enshrines an accidental inconsistency from the original code: the 'no bytes' placeholder does NOT word-wrap while the other two placeholder paths do, for no principled reason.",
    "failure_scenario": "A long merchant name in the 'image fetch returned null' case renders as a single clipped/overflowing line off the page edge, while the identical message in the missing-path and error cases wraps correctly. The flag freezes this divergence so it survives refactoring instead of being noticed and fixed; every future reader must guess whether the true/false split is meaningful. The helper should always wrap (one behavior), dropping the parameter."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/lib/services/ReconciliationService.ts",
    "line": 26,
    "summary": "The MatchingTolerances value object captures foreignCurrencyTolerancePercent (the outer match gate) as a configurable field, but the two related foreign-currency confidence bands FC_HIGH_CONFIDENCE_PERCENT (0.02) and FC_MEDIUM_CONFIDENCE_PERCENT (0.03) remain hardcoded module constants used inside scoreForeignCurrencyCandidate (lines 405-416). The value object only half-generalized the concept: one FC threshold is data, two are frozen.",
    "failure_scenario": "If a caller widens foreignCurrencyTolerancePercent (e.g. to 0.10 for volatile currencies), pairs up to 10% apart now match but are ALL scored 'near/low' because the 0.02/0.03 confidence bands never move with it — the tolerance and its confidence scale silently disagree. The tolerance knob looks configurable but produces incoherent confidence, and the two constants can't be reached through the value object, defeating the point of introducing it."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/lib/services/PDFExportService.ts",
    "line": 303,
    "summary": "The long generateReceiptPDF was split into drawSummaryHeader / drawCategoryTable / drawExpenseRow / drawGrandTotal, all sharing a single mutable PageCursor ({page, y}) that each method mutates in place, plus ensureRoom() which silently swaps cursor.page mid-draw. drawCategoryTable additionally BOTH mutates the cursor AND returns a subtotal (heterogeneous mutate+return contract).",
    "failure_scenario": "The sub-methods are not independently reasoning-safe: correctness depends on every helper both reading and writing cursor.y in the right order and remembering to call ensureRoom before drawing. A future edit that draws before checking room, or forgets a helper mutates cursor.page, produces content written past the page margin or onto a stale page — a class of bug that was impossible to introduce piecemeal when it was one linear method. The hidden shared state trades a long-but-linear method for several short methods coupled through an invisible channel."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/lib/services/ReconciliationService.ts",
    "line": 373,
    "summary": "scoreCandidate has an early guard `if (isForeignCurrency && !receipt.exchange_rate_to_cad) return null` (line 369), then the amount computation re-tests the same condition: `isForeignCurrency && receipt.exchange_rate_to_cad ? rawReceiptAmount * receipt.exchange_rate_to_cad : rawReceiptAmount` (lines 374-377). The second check is dead — the rate is already known non-null for foreign currency here — but is required only because TS narrowing was defeated by the `!` guard shape.",
    "failure_scenario": "Residue from the extraction: a reader can't tell whether the ternary's re-check guards a real case or is redundant, and the CAD branch and FC branch of the ternary look symmetric when they are not. Low cost, but it signals the guard/compute split wasn't cleaned — restructuring so isForeignCurrency implies a validated rate (e.g. compute receiptAmountCAD once above) would remove the double condition."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/lib/services/ExpenseService.ts",
    "line": 104,
    "summary": "The row typing was tightened with an ExpenseReceiptRow interface, but access still routes through `(data ?? []) as unknown as ExpenseReceiptRow[]` (line 104) and, in getVendorRule, `data.categories as unknown as { name: string } | null` (line 324). The join shape (1:1 embedded object vs PostgREST's array inference) is asserted by double-cast at each call site rather than encoded once in a typed query helper.",
    "failure_scenario": "Every place that reads the embedded expense_categories→categories join must repeat the same `as unknown as` incantation and inline comment. If the query's select shape changes, the casts keep compiling against the wrong runtime shape (arrays vs object) and fail silently at runtime. The extraction moved the mapping into mapReceiptRowToExpense but left the unsafe shape-coercion scattered instead of centralizing it in one typed boundary."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/lib/services/ReceiptService.ts",
    "line": 512,
    "summary": "computeRemainingAmount is named as a pure computation but also performs validation that throws ('Cannot split a receipt with no amount', 'Split amount must be less than the receipt total'). The split flow now has validation in three places: validateSplitAmount (static), validateSplitDate (static), and this throwing 'compute' helper — the amount-vs-total rule lives apart from the other split-amount validation in validateSplitAmount.",
    "failure_scenario": "A maintainer calling computeRemainingAmount expecting a pure number gets thrown exceptions; and someone hardening split validation will find/patch validateSplitAmount but miss the total-relative rule buried in a 'compute' method. The name hides a side-effecting responsibility, so the validation surface is fragmented and easy to update incompletely."
  }
]
```

Overall the refactor is mostly at the right depth: the MatchingTolerances value object is cleanly threaded through ReconciliationService, the `invertSign` flag argument was correctly eliminated in BaseCSVParser (replaced by the `expensesArePositive` subclass property — all 7 parser callers updated, no residual flag), DI conversion is consistent, and the ReceiptService/ExpenseService method extractions are sound. The findings above are the spots where a flag, a frozen constant, or shared mutable state indicates the extraction stopped one level short of fully generalizing the mechanism.
